A||N||9||ur2biteme||news on a possible saturn mid engine 2 seater entry.....||||1||
Z||000000||ur2biteme||02-25-2001||03:43 AM||||well, i know you guys might think this is a hoax, but according to my roomate (a saturn salesman) this might just be the real deal.  the Saturn dealer here's sales have been slipping for a while, and a new guy came in, reorganized, and doubled the sales in his first month.  in turn, he got to demo a not-yet-production vehicle.  the only conditions were that he could not have ANY cameras ANYWHERE near this vehicle, much less take any.<P>you'll have to bear with me, my roomate isnt exactly too mechanically minded, but i think it's using either a variant of the 3.0 v6 saturn is allready using, or an exclusive v6 that pulls somewhere in the ballpark of 300hp.  it's a plastic bodied mid engined 2 seater.  it's supposedly being named for the time being, the "2004 saturn speedster".  it is supposedly based from the opel.  <P>now, i did not see it for myself, but my roomate wouldnt have any reason to make this crap up either.  i'm giving him the benifit of the doubt for the time being.  now, hypothetically speaking, this would follow true to a rumor i heard a while back that saturn and pontiac were bidding for the north american rights to produce this car.  i'm guessing saturn won.  here's a bit of speculation on my part: maybe there was a comprimise.  we all know how there is nameplate swaping.  the geo/chevy prism-toyota corola for example.  now, if the two divisions struck up a comprimise, there may be 2 fiero-like 2 seaters under 2 seperate nameplates due up for 2004.  then again, this could be just another concept car that may never see the light of day.  who knows?  only time will tell.  <P>&lt;cautiously wonders if he'll need a flame suit&gt;<P>------------------<BR>Bob's '87 econo coupe<BR>aproaching 171k :)<BR> <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/ur2biteme" TARGET=_blank>a little piece of my madness</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||LZeitgeist||02-25-2001||10:17 AM||||I have a feeling that if anyone is going to do it, odds are it will be Saturn... they need to expand their product line (and *please*, no more frickin' SUV's!!!!), and they seem to be the type of company to try new things not currently on the market...<P>Now that they've got a good financial base to work from, maybe they'll give it a shot...<P>I'm not holding my breath, but I'll cross a couple fingers for them... it'd be great to have a new "Fiero", even if it's named something completely different... ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||02-25-2001||01:39 PM||||No flames from me. I think it's possible. Maybe even more likely than the Pontiac version, for a lot of reasons. Time will tell...<BR>Would still like to see an LS1-powered evolution (there's that word.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> ) of the 88 chassis, though.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 02-25-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000003||Black88GT||02-25-2001||02:01 PM||||Here we go again ...<P>My brothers friends cousins sisters aunts half brother works for GM and HE says that GM is making a new FIERO with a 1,000 Hp V8, yeah thats right, 7,000 HP!!!  [insert link to fake pictures here] Check it out its awesome!  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||02-25-2001||02:28 PM||||Yes there have been rumors of the Opel Speedster being brought to th U.S.<BR>Saturn would be the most likely outlet due to it's marketing abilities and excellent customer service/satisfaction. I don't think the production cars would have a 300hp V-6. They'll most likely have the 16-valve 4-cyl. They won't call it a Fiero. Saturn Speedster actually sounds good.<BR>Pontiac is looking to bring out a performance car once the F-bodies are gone. When the F-bodies disappear isn't known. There are many rumors floating around. In Car Craft magazine it was rumored that GM will still sell the F-bodies until sales drop below some preset level But they will be the same cars as today. In other words they won't spend any money to improve/upgrade the cars. There was a rumor of bringing back the "Banshee" name in a Corvette clone. (I highly doubt Chevy will allow that.)<BR>The chance that the Fiero name will be used for any new GM car is very unlikely. If a car gets bad press,  GM won't use the name again. (Actually this is the same for all the car manufacturers) <BR>Pontiac a while back was thinking of using the LeMans name plate for the same vehicle as the Malibu. But GM corporate didn't remember the LeMans of old they remembered the little Korean POS that they tried to pawn off to the public. Chevy actually toyed with the Nova nameplate but chose Malibu for the same reason.<BR>So you won't see any cars named:<BR>LeMans<BR>Nova<BR>Tempest<BR>Vega<BR>Pinto<BR>Maverick<BR>Corvair<BR>chevette<BR>Tempo<BR>Bobcat<BR>Omni<BR>Edsel<P>(Chevelle and GTO won't be used due to the extreme popularity of the older vehicles)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Slammed Fiero||02-25-2001||02:58 PM||||I worked for GM marketing for a year , it would NEVER happen. There's no way they give salesmen prototypes.<P><BR>Most prototypes are not road legal , missing safety equipment , side impact beams ect. There were tons of cars that we weren't allowed to drive off the property for that reason. For those of you in the Oshawa Ontario or Detroit area go look in the impounds , you will see lots of cars with production variances , they usually end up test mules for new parts / ideas. At the oshawa head office these cars are in plain view to the public , they will likley never leave the lot , In Detroit there are a few lots you can go spy on , if anyone is interested I can get you a couple different lot locations.  Also if your near Toronto  you can go to the VW head office in pickering , lots of neat non production VW's behind the main building , no fences , drive in take pics. A couple VR6 Beetles, even a convert , decals in plain view stating that the vehicle is not to leave the lot. <P><BR>300Hp from 3.0ltrs , that doesn't sound Like GM technology. Or better yet , use of available technology<P>another problem is that Any saturn speedster is going to be based on the Opel speedster , which isn't even out yet overseas as a production car, it's based somewhat on the lotus elise , which isn't available in North America for safety reasons. (But they show up at autocrosses) The engine bay of the Opel is tight , as is the elise , how you would ever fit a V6 in it without structural modification is beyond me. <P>The biggest problem is that GM cares very little about their dealers, At the head office most people have rarely ever set foot in a dealership, Turn the sales of a dealership around you may get a TV , or a Trip somewhere....<P>It's a nice thought ..I won't even get into the complications with Corvette...300hp mid engine car that will no doubt cost less than a vette, coming from saturn , be lighter and go faster..me thinks not.<P><BR>I bet even a base opel speedster would topple a Vette on a roadcourse..it weighs 1500lbs less. That is if it's as closely based on the Elise as I have read.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||02-25-2001||03:26 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>...If a car gets bad press,  GM won't use the name again. (Actually this is the same for all the car manufacturers) <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What if it's just an outright sales disaster?<BR>Now that they're "facelifting" the Aztec, I wonder if they'll rename it, too. <BR>Think they'll fool anybody?<BR>And... If the redesign becomes a hot seller, will the first "ugly" version become collectible, because it's so ummmm... uncommon?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>I have also heard that in Spanish speaking countries, "Nova" translates into "won't go", or some such.<BR>Matador means, literally, "bringer of death".<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 02-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||kslish||02-26-2001||01:20 AM||||Okay, for the sake of argument, let's say this guy is some kind of all-access god in Saturn land and this prototype is real.  Here is my best guess at what this all boils down to:<P>Here is the Lotus Elise that has been in production since 1996 and is the car the Opel Speedster is based on:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.automotive-technology.com/projects/elise/images/img3.jpg">  <P>Like SlammedFiero mentioned, this car (or the Opel for that matter) isn't designed to pass U.S. safety regulations so that limits the possibility of Saturn slapping their name on it for sale in the States without spending major $$$ to redesign the car to pass crash tests.  I doubt very much that GM would spend that kind of money right now on such a limited production auto.<P>Which brings us to this car:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cardesignnews.com/events/birmingham2000/preview/lotus-elise/images300/lotus-elise01s.jpg">  <P>This is the redesigned 2001 Lotus Elise which reportedly will eventually pass U.S. saftey regs and will be sold in the States since Lotus really needs something besides the Esprit to sell to maintain a worthwhile presence here.  <P>Could this be the car that Saturn may be looking to rebody/rebadge and sell since Lotus will be absorbing the cost of getting this new Elise U.S. certified?  <P>This new version of the Elise also has a longer wheelbase to increase interior room. (maybe that extra wheelbase could also be used to shoehorn in a V6 eventually)?  <P>Only time will tell if this guy's story and my hypothesis will turn out to be true....but it's fun to dream.<P>One things for sure, it won't be anything like the Fiero we love and drive, but more like a Toyota MR2 Spyder killer.<P>Ken S.<BR>'87 GT and '86 SC<p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 02-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||02-26-2001||01:54 AM||||Saturn won't buy the car and slap their name on it. Tney will build it on the chassis and make it U.S. safety compliant. Then they'll make the body with their styling and sell it.<BR>It'll most likely weigh more due to the U.S. crash specs. I doubt if it will be as fast with the extra weight and like I said they will use the 16-valve 4-cyl. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||ur2biteme||02-26-2001||04:54 AM||||for sake of argument, let's also remember that i'm not sure i believe the story.  i'm not sure i dont believe the story.  this is info from my roommate.  i havent seen it with my own eyes, but my roomate has no reason to lie.  however, this does seem to be a possibility.  <P>there are some obvious things that would have to happen.  this is, once again, speculation on my part.  <P>prototypes rarely make the market without DRASTIC changes.  i dont think we're gonna see a 1500 lb., 300 h.p. "elise of a different name".  what we'd probably see is a car based from the speedster, which is based from the elise.  we're talking about reverse engineering a car twice here.  i'm guessing with safety standards that it'll be somewhere around 2000-2300 lbs. roughly.  more than likely running the saturn 4, with the 6 as an option, but no 300 hp unit.  probably more in the ballpark of 180-220 hp out of the 6.  i'd love to see another fiero, but odds are, saturns gonna get it and keep it.  that is, even if it makes it to production.  <P>the F body is being killed as we all know.  the mustang is still selling, as are all these friggin imports i see running around.  there is a market for this vehicle, that is, if it's toned down and made safe.  even if they do the work to make it street legal, it will probably be lighter than most of the cars in it's class (audi TT anyone?).  as for the argument that it'll kill the 'vette because of a lower price, well, the lower level vettes i've seen arent all that pricey.  pardon my ignorance about corvettes, but i think you can pick one up for around $30k, right?  it may not be a zo6, but i'm sure it's no slouch. on top of that, can we be so sure that this car wont start at $30k?  i'm sure that there is alot of development costs (as stated before) that may or may not be passed on to the consumer.  remember, more than likely this is'nt going to be another fiero. <P>all i'm trying to say is that it's possible that saturn may just wind up having a mid engine 2 seater in 2004.  maybe not the "saturn speedster" my roomate "saw", but something a little more, well, for lack of a better word, realistic.  <P>now where is my flame suit.......    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P><p>[This message has been edited by ur2biteme (edited 02-26-2001).]||||reg||1
